Carafe: with FlagsWithoutFlagM, FEX spends fewer instructions on x86 flags on hosts without FlagM, like the A57.
x86 and Arm keep the carry of a subtraction in opposite forms. With FlagM, FEX fixes the form with CFINV, which the
dead-flag pass drops when carry is dead; without it the fix was a read, XOR and write of NZCV the pass could not
drop. With the option, the fix-up is a CarryInvert op lowered to that sequence, pending flags fold into conditional
and plain jumps instead of being stored before them, single carry bits are inserted with one ORN, dead NZCV inserts
(inc and dec keeping CF) are dropped, and a carry inversion after TEST or AND becomes a compare that yields carry in
the needed form. Off by default; with the option off FEX emits what it did before.
Checked in the study with FEX's ASM tests (32 and 64 bit, FlagM on and off) and 18000 differential fuzzer runs; it
cut ARM64 by 1.4 % in DXVK's d3d9.dll and executed instructions by 3.5 % in flag-heavy code.
MIT, as the files it changes.

